-## Subtopics
-
-### Multi-scale Voxel Representation
-Uses low-resolution voxels for coarse geometry and high-resolution for details. Low-res guides early training, high-res refines later. [Link](6269-regularization-and-geometry-constraints.md) (Parent)
-
-### Novel View Regularization
-Often combined with cross-scale adaptation to prevent local minima and overfitting. [Link](6269-regularization-and-geometry-constraints.md) (Parent)
+*   **Camera Poses:** The method relies on accurate camera poses. In scenarios with significant viewpoint changes or sparse training views, generalization may be challenging [S1].
+*   **Local Minima:** Omitting novel view rays risks local minima, causing incorrect geometry [S1].
+*   **Scale Mismatches:** Using only depth priors ($\mathcal{L}_d$) can suffer from higher error later in training due to scale mismatches, whereas $\mathcal{L}_{geo}$ refines depth progressively [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Adds overhead for reprojection and error computation across all scales for each training ray. The sources do not provide specific timing metrics relative to base training.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing multi-scale voxel grids. The sources do not specify memory overhead beyond the voxel structures themselves.
-*   **Performance:** Significantly reduces floaters and improves convergence quality across scales compared to training without geometric adaptation [S1]. Achieves lower final depth error than depth prior $\mathcal{L}_d$ alone [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Self-adaptive; no complex scheduling required; robust to geometric noise compared to feature-warping methods like GeCoNeRF [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on accurate camera poses. Performance may degrade in few-shot scenarios with significant viewpoint changes or sparse training views [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Measured Performance:** In FrugalNeRF, $\mathcal{L}_{geo}$-only achieves lower final depth error compared to depth prior $\mathcal{L}_d$ alone [S1]. Combining $\mathcal{L}_{geo}$ and $\mathcal{L}_d$ yields the best results [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Significantly reduces floaters, improves convergence quality across scales, and provides a stronger and more robust constraint than depth priors [S1]. It eliminates the need for complex scheduling and ground truth pose sampling [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Few-shot NeRF methods relying on this may face challenges in generalization with sparse training views or significant viewpoint changes [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Cross-scale geometric adaptation was proposed in **FrugalNeRF** (2025) by the authors of [S1]. It is designed for extreme few-shot novel view synthesis, accelerating training and regularizing geometry without external priors. It builds on the concept of weight-sharing voxels across scales to capture varying scene frequencies [S1].
+*   **Origin:** Proposed in FrugalNeRF [S1].
+*   **History:** The method was introduced to accelerate and regularize training for extreme few-shot novel view synthesis without learned priors [S1]. It addresses the issue of geometric inconsistency and floaters common in few-shot scenarios [S1].
+*   **Usage:** Used in FrugalNeRF for few-shot NeRF training [S1].
 
